sport and reuse】\nL20:     5. 【10†2.5 Formal results as a design basis】\nL21:     6. 【11†2.6 What the application requests mean】\nL22:     7. 【12†2.7 Serving components and modes】\nL23:   4. 【13†3 Experimental design】\nL24:     1. 【14†3.1 Fixed source and separate cohorts】\nL25:     2. 【15†3.2 External comparison: what was and was not established】\nL26:     3. 【16†3.3 Same-driver memory contrast】\nL27:     4. 【17†3.4 Arrival and acceptance endpoint】\nL28:       1. 【18†Why offered rates are controlled.】\nL29:     5. 【19†3.5 Statistics and clocks】\nL30:       1. 【20†Reading the main contrasts.】\nL31:   5. 【21†4 Memory, scale and result preservation】\nL32:   6. 【22†5 Completed computation and reusable structure】\nL33:   7. 【23†6 Serving profiles, capacity and configuration choices】\nL34:     1. 【24†6.1 A confirmed 48-core baseline】\nL35:     2. 【25†6.2 Equal-total-resource deployment】\nL36:     3. 【26†6.3 Fine transitions】\nL37:     4. 【27†6.4 192-core confirmation at 1,000 and 1,150 requests/s】\nL38:     5. 【28†6.5 Capacity of one 192-core worker and the network bound】\nL39:     6. 【29†6.6 Overload and memory bounds】\nL40:     7. 【30†6.7 Host size: 48, 96 and 192 cores】\nL41:     8. 【31†6.8 Where the time went】\nL42:   8. 【32†7 What release 1.2 changes for an integrating service】\nL43:   9. 【33†8 Implications and validity】\nL44:     1. 【34†8.1 Application-facing meaning of the nine mechanisms】\nL45:       1. 【35†Reproducibility.】\nL46:       2. 【36†Data availability.】\nL47:       3. 【37†Assistance and responsibility.】\nL48:   10. 【38†9 Conclusion】\nL49:   11. 【39†References】\nL50:   12. 【40†A Complete detailed evidence atlas】\nL51: 【41†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL52: \nL53: arXiv:2610.14849v2 [cs.CR] 26 Oct 2026\nL54: # StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a\nL55: Reusable Sparse GKR Engine Thanks: This version evaluates StateSync-GKR release 1.2.0 against same-day measurements of release 1.1. Version 1 of this report (arXiv:2610.14849v1) evaluates release 1.1.0 and remains available.\nL56: \nL57: Jinwook Kim  (for the Oraclizer Core Team) Affiliation: Oraclizer Labs, Inc., Delaware, USA Affiliation: Oraclizer Labs Korea, Seoul, Korea Email: jay@oraclizer.io\nL58: \nL59: October 2026\nL60: ###### Abstract\nL61: Authenticated state transitions need proof engines that conserve memory, avoid repeated setup, keep every result bound to its request under parallel execution and return complete proofs at service rates. We present StateSync-GKR, a reusable Rust sumcheck/GKR core with a sparse-Merkle frontend, and evaluate release 1.2.0 against release 1.1 measured on the same day on the same instance types, request corpus and latency budgets.\nL62: Sparse layer reduction keeps additional requested allocation 3,242-fold and peak process memory about 89-fold below the engine’s dense reference at width 4,096.\nL63: Release 1.2 changes two parts of the software while proof bytes and verifier acceptance stay fixed: reorganized round arithmetic raises prepared proving throughput 2.4 to 2.7-fold on a 192-core bare-metal host, and a new serving path starts each job as soon as a worker is free, bounds queued work and buffered replies, and returns proofs in completion order bound to request identifiers. On a 192-core VM at 1,000 requests/s, 99th-percentile latency to verified acceptance falls from 440 to 34 ms.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.seas.upenn.edu/~leebcc/documents/mehrabi21-ispass.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.seas.upenn.edu/~leebcc/documents/mehrabi21-ispass.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching"
